  "title": "CHAPTER 2: GEOGRAPHY OF MAHARASHTRA",
  "order": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "52bpeky",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.1 Location and Extent"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Maharashtra comprises three main physiographic divisions: the Sahyadri Range (Western Ghats), the Konkan Coastal Strip, and the Deccan Plateau.",
        "The state's drainage system is broadly categorized into westward-flowing rivers emptying into the Arabian Sea and eastward-flowing rivers draining into the Bay of Bengal.",
        "Major eastward river systems include the Godavari and Krishna basins, which irrigate large parts of the Maharashtra Plateau.",
        "Maharashtra is categorized into 6 major administrative revenue divisions encompassing distinct ecological and economic zones."
      ],
      "id": "e95jrgn"
    },
